<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An LLM agent’s effectiveness depends not only on its underlying model, but also on its <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">harness</em>: the prompts, memory, skills, tools, and orchestration through which the model performs tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">SST, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weng, 2026</a>)</cite>. Even a strong model can perform substantially differently under different harnesses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. As agents expand beyond coding into office workflows and high-stakes domains such as legal work, harness design must accommodate increasingly diverse tasks and operational requirements <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harvey AI, 2026</a>)</cite>. Manually designing and refining harnesses for these demands requires repeated engineering effort, motivating recursive self-improvement on harness design <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qian et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weng, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Recursive self-improvement (RSI) pursues AI that improves AI: a system autonomously makes persistent changes to itself, and these changes further shape how later improvements are generated, evaluated, and retained <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Duan et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Early harness RSI methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2026</a>)</cite> focus on optimizing candidate components against fixed training tasks, unit tests, or validation feedback, selecting the best configuration before deployment. However, a static harness selected beforehand often fails to generalize to the diverse, unpredictable environments encountered in the open world <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>, a dynamic setting where agents continually encounter new tasks with diverse and evolving requirements. To address this, researchers have moved harness RSI to the <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">open world</em>, allowing systems to dynamically adapt to newly arriving tasks. Current open-world methods typically address this by recursively improving a harness for the current task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>, or by carrying a successful execution harness forward across successive task batches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, open-world harness evolution faces two connected challenges. First, tasks typically lack ground-truth labels or reliable verifiers. While existing methods rely on proxy signals, assumed ground truth answers, or pairwise output judgments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, safely using imperfect execution evidence to guide harness changes remains difficult. Second, improving a harness for one task does not automatically improve how the next task is refined. Task-specific edits often fail when requirements change, and directly inheriting a highly tailored execution harness can cause noisy recursive improvement as task distributions shift. The refinement process itself, however, produces highly reusable experience: how a weakness is diagnosed, which changes are tested, and what their execution reveals. Therefore, the improvement process itself must become a source of learning, enabling the system to recursively upgrade its own refinement mechanism. This leads to our central question:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="42.74" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 676.77 42.74" width="676.77"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,42.74) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(40.61,0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#ECF1F8;" fill="#ECF1F8"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 2.08 -2.08 L 2.08 40.67 L 597.64 40.67 L 597.64 -2.08 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F4F8FE;" fill="#F4F8FE" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 42.74 L 595.56 42.74 L 595.56 0 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 13.84 9.61)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:40.15em;--ltx-fo-height:1.89em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="28.9" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 26.21)" width="555.56"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S1.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:40.15em;">
<span id="S1.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Can recursive self-improvement improve both the current harness 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S1.p4.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#174EA6;">and how future harnesses are improved?</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, we introduce <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="S1.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S1.p5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span>, a plug-and-play framework for <em id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">recursive agent harnessing</em> in an open world. We use <em id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">harnessing</em> to describe the process of optimizing the runtime scaffolding around a frozen base model to enable it to act as an autonomous agent. <span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="S1.p5.1.4.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S1.p5.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span> improves the harness for the current task and extracts lessons from that process to guide future harnessing. It implements these updates through two coupled levels of recursion: ❶ <span id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task-level recursion</span> refines the current task’s <em id="S1.p5.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">execution harness</em>. To overcome the absence of ground-truth feedback, we introduce a contrastive <em id="S1.p5.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Propose–Probe–Compose</em> step: it proposes harness edits, tests their effects under matched conditions, and composes changes supported by behavioral contrasts. Building on this, <em id="S1.p5.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">parallel recursion</em> explores multiple candidates simultaneously and composes changes from their execution evidence, while <em id="S1.p5.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sequential recursion</em> builds successive revisions iteratively. ❷ <span id="S1.p5.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Harnessing-level recursion</span> uses the resulting task-level explorations to update a <em id="S1.p5.1.11" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">continual improvement harness</em>. Instead of inheriting the previous execution harness, this improver selectively retains reusable components and lessons about when and why they worked, guiding future proposals and composition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="S1.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S1.p6.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span> on WorkBuddy-Bench, JobBench, and Harvey’s Legal Agent Benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harvey AI, 2026</a>)</cite> across four executor–harness configurations. One contrastive step improves performance on all three benchmarks in every configuration, with gains of up to <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11.5</annotation></semantics></math> points on WorkBuddy-Bench, <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.5</annotation></semantics></math> points on JobBench, and <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11.8</annotation></semantics></math> percentage points in LAB Partial pass rate. Parallel recursion adds up to <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.9</annotation></semantics></math> points on WorkBuddy-Bench, <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.2</annotation></semantics></math> on JobBench, and <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.7</annotation></semantics></math> percentage points on LAB Partial (Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>); the budget study shows gains from both parallel and sequential recursion. Importantly, task-level exploration also benefits subsequent harnessing. Libraries accumulated with a larger recursion budget support stronger subsequent harness edits even without additional probing, and a library transferred from JobBench improves performance on WorkBuddy-Bench (Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4 Analysis ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). Our contributions are threefold:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.2em;">
<li id="S1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">①</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Recursive agent harnessing:</span>
<span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span> refines the current execution harness and learns from the
refinement process to guide future harnessing.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">②</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task-level recursion without ground-truth feedback:</span>
We introduce a contrastive Propose–Probe–Compose step that supports
both parallel and sequential recursion.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">③</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental results:</span>
We demonstrate consistent gains across three benchmarks and four
executor–harness configurations, including settings where the same
model serves as both executor and editor.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.14224v1/components.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/171;" width="476" height="171" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S1.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The eight harness components in <span id="S1.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="S1.F2.5.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S1.F2.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span>, grouped by their role in the runtime.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="S2.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first define recursive agent harnessing and its open-world setting
(§<a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Recursive Agent Harnessing ‣ 2 Harness2 ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>). We then present task-level recursion for refining
the execution harness (§<a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Task-Level Recursion ‣ 2 Harness2 ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>), followed by
harnessing-level recursion for updating the improvement harness across
tasks (§<a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Harnessing-Level Recursion ‣ 2 Harness2 ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Recursive Agent Harnessing</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Harness Configuration and Setting.</span>
Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> be a model with frozen parameters. A <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">harness</em>
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}=(c^{1},\ldots,c^{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}=(c^{1},\ldots,c^{K})</annotation></semantics></math> consists of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> editable components,
with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denoting component <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,\ldots,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,\ldots,K\}</annotation></semantics></math>. It
composes with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to form an agent
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\cdot;\mathcal{M},\mathcal{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\cdot;\mathcal{M},\mathcal{H})</annotation></semantics></math> without changing its
weights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qian et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
For the execution harness, we adopt the file-level decomposition of
AHE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2026</a>)</cite> and extend it by one component, exposing
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=8</annotation></semantics></math> components: a system prompt,
guardrails, memory, skills, tool guidance, scripts, subagents, and a
middleware plugin. Each component is mounted as a file in the runtime.
Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows their runtime roles, and
Table <a href="#A4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ D.3 Editable Harness Components ‣ Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS3" title="D.3 Editable Harness Components ‣ Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.3</span></a> lists their
files, activation, and definitions. We study harness evolution over a
stream of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> open-world tasks <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1},\ldots,q_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1},\ldots,q_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, without ground-truth
answers or labeled validation feedback.
Each task starts from the common execution harness <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The pipeline may rerun the current task within a bounded budget before
returning its final deliverable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Recursive Agent Harnessing.</span>
Recursive self-improvement (RSI) is the capability of an AI system to turn
execution outcomes and internal reflections into persistent changes to
itself, such that these changes also affect how later improvements are
generated, evaluated, and retained <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Duan et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. The object of such
changes varies: model weights, as in self-rewarding language
models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; improvement programs, as in
STOP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zelikman et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; and agent code, as in the Darwin
Gödel Machine <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>. The agent harness can be such an
object as well. It is the system surrounding a base model that enables
it to act as an autonomous agent
(<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Agent}=\text{Model}+\text{Harness}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Agent</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Model</mtext><mo>+</mo><mtext>Harness</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Agent}=\text{Model}+\text{Harness}</annotation></semantics></math>), without changing the
model weights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weng, 2026</a>)</cite>. We call the process of optimizing
this layer <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">harnessing</em>. Rather than only revising an execution harness under
a fixed improvement procedure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>,
we also update the harness guiding future revisions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.SS1.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="138.65" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 676.77 138.65" width="676.77"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,138.65) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F1F6FE;" fill="#F1F6FE" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.15 L 0 125.76 C 0 128.06 1.86 129.91 4.15 129.91 L 672.62 129.91 C 674.91 129.91 676.77 128.06 676.77 125.76 L 676.77 4.15 C 676.77 1.86 674.91 0 672.62 0 L 4.15 0 C 1.86 0 0 1.86 0 4.15 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F1F6FE;" fill="#F1F6FE" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.15 L 0 125.76 C 0 128.06 1.86 129.91 4.15 129.91 L 672.62 129.91 C 674.91 129.91 676.77 128.06 676.77 125.76 L 676.77 4.15 C 676.77 1.86 674.91 0 672.62 0 L 4.15 0 C 1.86 0 0 1.86 0 4.15 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#1A73E8;--ltx-fill-color:#1A73E8;--ltx-fg-color:#1A73E8;" color="#1A73E8" fill="#1A73E8" stroke="#1A73E8" stroke-width="2.5pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 1.73 129.91 L 1.73 0"></path></g><g transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.45 121.61)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_nestedsvg" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#174EA6;" fill="#174EA6" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 17.03 L 214.95 17.03 L 214.95 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#174EA6;" fill="#174EA6" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 17.03 L 214.95 17.03 L 214.95 0 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 8.3 5.75)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:16.32em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:8.8pt;" height="9.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 7.69)" width="198.68"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S2.SS1.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Definition: Recursive Agent Harnessing</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 16.6 16.53)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:46.51em;--ltx-fo-height:6.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="95.32" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 92.63)" width="643.57"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S2.SS1.p3.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:46.51em;">
<span id="S2.SS1.p3.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.SS1.p3.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Recursive agent harnessing</em><span id="S2.SS1.p3.pic1.2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> couples two recursive updates:
<span id="S2.SS1.p3.pic1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">task-level recursion</span> refines the current execution harness
using generated harnesses and execution evidence;
<span id="S2.SS1.p3.pic1.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">harnessing-level recursion</span> updates the improvement harness
that guides this refinement, using adopted harnesses and
improvement experience after each task to inform subsequent
refinement.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Recursive agent harnessing thus extends the model–harness view
from an agent that executes tasks to an agent that also learns
how to improve its harness: the execution harness is the system
being improved, and the improvement harness is the mechanism
that improves it. In <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span>, as shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.2.1 A Contrastive Improvement Step ‣ 2.2 Task-Level Recursion ‣ 2 Harness2 ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the
<em id="S2.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">execution harness</em> is refined for the
current task, while the <em id="S2.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">continual improvement harness</em>
supports refinement across tasks. The improvement harness
remains fixed during task-level recursion and is updated
afterward through its <em id="S2.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">harness improvement library</em>.
This library retains adopted harnesses together with lessons
from their refinement, making both available for reuse and
adaptation in subsequent proposals and composition.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Task-Level Recursion</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Task-level recursion aims to improve the execution harness for the
current task. The challenge in our open-world setting is to guide
revisions without ground-truth feedback. We therefore introduce a
<em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">contrastive improvement step</em> that proposes changes, executes
them, and examines their behavioral consequences. Building on this
step, parallel and sequential recursion use the generated harnesses
and execution evidence for further refinement.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.1 </span>A Contrastive Improvement Step</h4>

<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.14224v1/harness2_main_new.png" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/152;" width="476" height="152" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S2.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The <span id="S2.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="S2.F3.5.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S2.F3.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span> pipeline. Task-level recursion proposes invariant and
domain-specific harness edits, probes their effects, and composes a successor
execution harness. After each task, harnessing-level recursion updates the
improvement harness with reusable harnesses and lessons from the refinement
process.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A contrastive step consists of <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Propose</span>, <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Probe</span>, and
<span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compose</span>. Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=(\tau,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=(\tau,y)</annotation></semantics></math> denote an execution record, with
trajectory <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and deliverable <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. The step applies an improvement
operation <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> to the current execution harness <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> and
the available task-local execution and improvement history <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{H}^{\prime},e^{\prime},\xi)=\Phi(q_{t},\mathcal{H},\mathcal{E};\mathcal{R}_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{H}^{\prime},e^{\prime},\xi)=\Phi(q_{t},\mathcal{H},\mathcal{E};\mathcal{R}_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">It returns a successor harness <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, its execution record
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a step record <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> containing the reference executions,
candidate harnesses and probes, composition decisions, and successor
execution. The model and environment are omitted because they stay
unchanged; the improvement harness <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> also remains fixed
throughout the task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Propose.</span>
The pipeline first obtains a reference execution
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{0}\sim\mathcal{A}(q_{t};\mathcal{M},\mathcal{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{0}\sim\mathcal{A}(q_{t};\mathcal{M},\mathcal{H})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math> denotes sampling an execution from the agent. The reference reveals how the
harness behaves, not whether its deliverable is correct. Conditioned on
the task, current harness, reference, earlier history, and library in
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the pipeline proposes two candidates. The
<span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">invariant</span> candidate targets agentic deficiencies that may recur
across domains, such as planning, tool use, context management, or
delegation. The <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">domain-specific</span> candidate targets the knowledge,
procedures, and constraints of the current domain or environment.
Each candidate is an executable harness obtained by editing the current
harness’s component files. Proposals may reuse or adapt
existing harness components from the library, but task-specific facts
must be checked against the current task’s evidence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Probe.</span>
Each candidate harness is executed on the same task under matched
model and environment configurations. Comparing its trajectory and
deliverable with those of the reference execution yields a
<span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">behavioral contrast</span>, providing evidence of whether the edits
mitigate the targeted deficiency, leave the relevant behavior unchanged,
or introduce regressions. These contrasts inform subsequent composition
rather than verify task correctness, as execution stochasticity and
shared model biases limit the reliability of the evidence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compose.</span>
The pipeline reads the reference, candidate harnesses, their diffs
against the current harness, and all available task-local execution
evidence to form the successor <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Composition is selective: it may merge supported changes, revise
conflicting edits, adopt one candidate, or preserve the current harness.
Each decision is tied to a concrete observation rather than a presumed
score gain. The composed harness is then executed to produce <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the decisions and their evidence are recorded in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>.
The step therefore returns an executed successor together with the
evidence and decisions behind its refinement.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.2 </span>Parallel and Sequential Recursion</h4>

<figure id="S2.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.14224v1/scaling.png" id="S2.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/119;" width="476" height="119" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S2.F4.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Two forms of task-level recursion.
<span id="S2.F4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a) Parallel recursion</span> probes multiple candidates and independently
composes successor harnesses. <span id="S2.F4.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b) Sequential recursion</span> carries each refined harness and
accumulated execution evidence into the next step.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building on the contrastive improvement step, we introduce two
forms of task-level recursion (Figure <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.2.2 Parallel and Sequential Recursion ‣ 2.2 Task-Level Recursion ‣ 2 Harness2 ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>): <em id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">parallel recursion</em> composes
refinements from multiple candidate harnesses and their executions,
while <em id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sequential recursion</em> carries the refined harness and
execution evidence into subsequent steps. A positive integer <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>
controls the exploration budget: <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> candidates per scope and
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> compositions in parallel, or <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> chained steps in sequence.
The improvement harness <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> remains fixed throughout.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parallel Recursion.</span>
Parallel recursion follows the aggregation principle of RSA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Venkatraman et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, but operates on executable
harnesses and their behavioral consequences rather than reasoning
outputs. The pipeline collects <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> reference executions of the current
harness <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, then independently proposes and probes
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> invariant and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> domain-specific candidates. Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>
denote the reference executions and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> the pool of
candidate harnesses paired with their execution records.
Each of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> independent composition sessions receives the full pool:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\prime}_{j}=\textsc{Compose}_{j}(q_{t},\mathcal{H},\mathcal{B},\mathcal{P};\mathcal{R}_{t}),\qquad j=1,\ldots,k." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Compose</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\prime}_{j}=\textsc{Compose}_{j}(q_{t},\mathcal{H},\mathcal{B},\mathcal{P};\mathcal{R}_{t}),\qquad j=1,\ldots,k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Each session selectively combines or revises supported edits to
produce a successor <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\prime}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\prime}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is then executed.
The recursive update thus uses generated harnesses and execution
evidence for further refinement, not merely output ranking.
Our implementation performs one population update; <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> controls
its width rather than its depth.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sequential Recursion.</span>
Sequential recursion applies iterative self-refinement
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madaan et al., 2023</a>)</cite> to the execution harness.
Starting from <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{(0)}=\mathcal{H}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{(0)}=\mathcal{H}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, it chains <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>
contrastive steps, each conditioned on the preceding harness
and accumulated execution history <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{(r-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{(r-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\mathcal{H}^{(r)},e^{(r)},\xi^{(r)})=\Phi(q_{t},\mathcal{H}^{(r-1)},\mathcal{E}^{(r-1)};\mathcal{R}_{t}),\qquad r=1,\ldots,k." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{H}^{(r)},e^{(r)},\xi^{(r)})=\Phi(q_{t},\mathcal{H}^{(r-1)},\mathcal{E}^{(r-1)};\mathcal{R}_{t}),\qquad r=1,\ldots,k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is the contrastive step defined in
Equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.2.1 A Contrastive Improvement Step ‣ 2.2 Task-Level Recursion ‣ 2 Harness2 ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, returning the refined harness,
its execution record <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{(r)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{(r)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the step record <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi^{(r)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi^{(r)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The initial history contains <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> base executions and is extended
after each step with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi^{(r)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi^{(r)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Later steps can therefore retain,
revise, or correct earlier changes using both current and
previous evidence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Final Selection.</span>
For <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, an agentic selector compares anonymized deliverables
against the task request and source materials. It returns an
existing output without access to its originating harness or
synthesizing new content. The corresponding harness is denoted
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{t}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{t}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the star indicates adoption rather
than verified optimality. Selection adds no harness revision.
At <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>, both forms reduce to the basic contrastive step and
bypass selection. Appendix <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Prompts ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a> provides the stage prompts.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Harnessing-Level Recursion</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Harnessing-level recursion uses the experience of refining the
current execution harness to improve future harnessing. After each
task, the pipeline updates the library within the improvement
harness <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, making adopted harnesses and lessons from
their refinement available to subsequent tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reflect on the Improvement Process.</span>
After returning the deliverable, the pipeline reviews the complete
improvement record, denoted by <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. This record includes
reference executions, candidate harnesses and probes, composition
decisions, and the selected result. Reflection extracts lessons
about which changes addressed the observed deficiencies, under
what conditions, and with what consequences. Lessons are retained
only when supported by execution contrasts; selecting an output
alone does not establish transferability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Update the Improvement Harness.</span>
The pipeline retains the adopted harness <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{t}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{t}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>
and supported lessons in the <em id="S2.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">harness improvement library</em>.
We summarize reflection and the resulting library update as</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{t+1}=\textsc{Update}(\mathcal{R}_{t},\mathcal{H}_{t}^{\star},\mathcal{D}_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Update</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{t+1}=\textsc{Update}(\mathcal{R}_{t},\mathcal{H}_{t}^{\star},\mathcal{D}_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Each library entry contains an executable harness and an
<span id="S2.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EXPERIENCE.md</span> recording the applicable situation,
lessons, and supporting evidence. An index enables subsequent
sessions to inspect relevant entries on demand. Existing harnesses
provide components for direct reuse or adaptation, while the
accompanying experience guides their application. When evidence
is insufficient, reflection adds no new experience entry.
Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Improvement Sessions and Library Configuration ‣ Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a> describes the library organization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reuse in Subsequent Improvement.</span>
The next task starts from the common execution harness
<math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, but refinement is guided by the updated
improvement harness <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Earlier components are
selectively reused or adapted through Propose, rather than
automatically inherited; accumulated experience also informs
Probe and Compose. Model weights, stage instructions, and the
decision rule remain fixed, while the library evolves. Increasing task-level exploration produces additional execution
evidence and composition decisions from which future harnessing
can learn. Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4 Analysis ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> examines this connection using
libraries accumulated under different recursion budgets.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Experiment Setup</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmarks.</span>
We evaluate on three benchmarks from distinct professional domains.
<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">WorkBuddy-Bench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2026</a>)</cite> covers office-style deliverables
in three domains (Code, Web, Office). <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">JobBench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026</a>)</cite>
targets end-to-end professional job tasks with long executions.
<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LAB</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harvey AI, 2026</a>)</cite> is a legal-work benchmark; following its protocol we report <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Partial</em>, the
per-criterion pass rate. Main tables report means and standard deviations
over three runs, each with a shuffled task order; the
<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">+ Recursion</span> rows report changes in means.
We also report #Steps, the number of execution steps per task,
so that score changes can be read against how much execution they required. More details about the benchmarks are in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Benchmarks ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span>
The baselines cover offline harness optimization, retrieval-only reasoning memory, and
per-task test-time harness evolution. <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Base harness</em> runs the initial harness
unchanged. <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ReasoningBank</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2026b</a>)</cite> keeps the
harness fixed and retrieves distilled strategies from past trajectories back
into context. <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Meta-Harness</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2026b</a>)</cite> searches harness
code offline against a training split and deploys the best one.
<em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Harness Scaling</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026a</a>)</cite> revises the harness for
each evaluation instance by spending additional inference budget on revision
without carrying state across instances. More details about these baselines are in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Baselines ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation Details.</span>
Gemini 3.5 Flash <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Google DeepMind, 2026</a>)</cite> and GLM-5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2026</a>)</cite>
are frozen executors under the off-the-shelf OpenCode <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">SST, 2024</a>)</cite> and
Codex <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>)</cite> harnesses. The same improvement step applies to all four
configurations per benchmark. The default harness
editor is Claude Opus 4.8 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2026</a>)</cite> running under OpenCode. Main tables
report <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="S3.SS1.p3.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span> with a single improvement step (<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}1</annotation></semantics></math>);
<span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">+ Recursion</span> applies parallel task-level recursion at <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}5</annotation></semantics></math>. The
harness improvement library starts empty for each benchmark stream and is updated
after each task. Appendices <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> and <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Prompts ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>
provide implementation details and stage prompts.</p>
</div>
</section>
