<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> delivers consistent gains across models and benchmarks.</span></span>
A single improvement step improves both GLM-5 and Gemini 3.5 Flash under OpenCode and Codex. On WorkBuddy-Bench, <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span> raises GLM-5’s average score by 11.5 points under OpenCode, the largest one-step improvement over the base harness on this benchmark. The same pattern extends to JobBench and LAB, where gains reach 4.5 points and 11.8 percentage points in Partial pass rate, respectively. By contrast, ReasoningBank falls as much as 6.6 points below the base harness on coding tasks. Harness Scaling underperforms the base in seven of the twelve benchmark–configuration pairs. <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span> exceeds the compared baselines on WorkBuddy-Bench Avg. and JobBench Score in all four configurations; on LAB Partial, Meta-Harness leads for Gemini 3.5 Flash under Codex.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Weaker executors benefit more on WorkBuddy-Bench and LAB.</span>
Under both initial harnesses, GLM-5 receives larger absolute gains than Gemini 3.5 Flash on WorkBuddy-Bench and LAB. On WorkBuddy-Bench, a single step lifts GLM-5 to 75.7 with OpenCode and 75.3 with Codex, surpassing the corresponding base Gemini scores of 72.4 and 74.5. On JobBench, Gemini instead gains 4.5 and 1.4 points under OpenCode and Codex, compared with GLM-5’s 3.6 and 1.2. A weaker model with an improved harness can thus outperform a stronger one with its original harness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task-level recursion extends the gains across initial harnesses.</span>
Parallel task-level recursion at <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}5</annotation></semantics></math> adds a further 2.9 to 3.9 points on WorkBuddy-Bench, 3.0 to 4.2 points on JobBench, and 2.6 to 6.7 percentage points in LAB’s Partial pass rate over a single step. On JobBench, the initial OpenCode–Codex gaps of 2.4 points for Gemini and 3.0 for GLM-5 both shrink to 0.5. On WorkBuddy-Bench, Gemini’s gap falls from 2.1 to 0.4, whereas GLM-5’s increases from 0.2 to 1.3. Recursion therefore does not uniformly close the gap between initial harnesses.</p>
</div>
