<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="S4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span> along six lines: the effect of task-level recursion
under increasing budget; the compute–performance trade-off; an ablation of
the library, the scoped candidates, and composition; library generalization
without execution, across benchmarks, and across tasks; the same model as
improver and executor; and a case study. Additional analyses are presented
in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Supplementary Experiments and Analyses ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of Task-Level Recursion.</span>
Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4 Analysis ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> compares parallel and sequential recursion on WB-code and WB-office. Both improve monotonically as <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> increases from <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Parallel recursion rises from <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="76.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>76.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">76.5</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>80.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80.8</annotation></semantics></math> on Code and from <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="79.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>79.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">79.5</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="83.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>83.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">83.4</annotation></semantics></math> on Office. In contrast, ReasoningBank and Harness Scaling show smaller or inconsistent gains as <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> increases. Harness Scaling regresses from <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}2</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> on both domains, while ReasoningBank fluctuates. Notably, both recursive forms of <span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="S4.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.p3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span> at <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}2</annotation></semantics></math> outperform every plotted baseline at <math id="S4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}5</annotation></semantics></math> on both domains. Parallel recursion finishes above sequential recursion at <math id="S4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}5</annotation></semantics></math>, although sequential recursion is higher on Code at <math id="S4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}2</annotation></semantics></math>. Increasing test-time exploration alone therefore does not ensure better performance; recursive harness refinement produces more consistent gains in these settings. Parallel proposal and composition sessions can run concurrently; realized latency also depends on available concurrency and rollout duration.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:64%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.14224v1/total_cost.svg" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:310/123;" width="310" height="123"></object>
