<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Same Model as Executor and Editor.</span>
To test whether gains require a stronger editor, we use the same model
for both roles with OpenCode at <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}2</annotation></semantics></math> (Figure <a href="#S4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 4 Analysis ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).
All five models, from GLM-5 to Claude Opus 5, improve on every benchmark.
Opus 5 gains <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.5</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.0</annotation></semantics></math> points on WorkBuddy-Bench, JobBench,
and LAB; GLM-5 gains <math id="S4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>13.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13.7</annotation></semantics></math> points on LAB. These results demonstrate
that harness refinement need not rely on a stronger editor.
Appendix <a href="#A5.SS3" title="E.3 Transfer Across Executor Models ‣ Appendix E Supplementary Experiments and Analyses ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.3</span></a> evaluates transfer to other executor models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case Study.</span>
Figure <a href="#S4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 4 Analysis ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> presents a JobBench task evaluated using Gemini 3.5 Flash and OpenCode (additional examples across all benchmarks and domains are available in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS7" title="E.7 Qualitative Case Studies ‣ Appendix E Supplementary Experiments and Analyses ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.7</span></a>). The baseline run scored <math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="43.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>43.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">43.1</annotation></semantics></math>. Its trajectory revealed procedural failures rather than knowledge gaps: data was never reconciled across source files, and outputs were never reopened for verification after being written. To resolve this, two targeted interventions were applied. First, the invariant intervention acts as a process-level guardrail, forcing the model to inventory all inputs, trace figures to their exact sources, and use a verification script to reopen and check produced files. Second, the domain intervention introduces a specialized skill providing reconciliation mechanics for schedule, venue, labor, and budget files, alongside tool guidance for handling specific number formats. When evaluated against the reference, the Composer retained all four edits introduced by the two interventions. The optimized re-run successfully cross-referenced the figures and restored the memo’s missing sections, raising the score to <math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="77.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>77.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">77.8</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
