<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Recursive Self-Improvement.</span>
Recursive self-improvement (RSI) envisions AI systems that participate
in their own development, with improvements to their capabilities
enabling further improvements. The central ambition is to make progress
cumulative: a better system should also become better at producing
its successors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weng, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Duan et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
The object of improvement can be model weights, as in
self-rewarding language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>,
or the programs and harnesses surrounding a frozen model.
Early approaches refine outputs through self-feedback or retain
reflections for subsequent attempts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madaan et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Moving beyond individual outputs, STOP recursively
rewrites a code-improvement program <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zelikman et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, while the
Darwin Gödel Machine evolves agent code through benchmark-evaluated
variants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>. Hyperagents further makes
its meta-level modification procedure editable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026c</a>)</cite>,
while MetaSkill-Evolve and MCE co-evolve task
artifacts and the skills governing their construction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
These systems advance RSI through utility-, benchmark-, or
validation-guided optimization. <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="S5.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.p1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span> instead studies how harness
improvement can accumulate across a deployment task stream without
ground-truth feedback or revisiting completed tasks. Task-specific
refinement contributes reusable harnesses and experiences to the
improver’s harness guiding subsequent improvement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Harness Optimization and Evolution.</span>
Automatic optimization has expanded from prompts, agent
workflows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khattab et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agrawal et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, memories and skills <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2026</a>)</cite> to complete executable
harnesses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
Offline methods search over candidate harnesses using reusable
training tasks and evaluation feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026e</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026d</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
Related approaches use task rewards to train harness editors or
generators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>, or collect
diagnostic experience through labeled search to guide later
adaptation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>.
Offline preparation can also be followed by online adaptation.
Test-time methods refine a task-specific harness through execution
feedback or carry an evolving harness across unlabeled
batches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>.
Persistent improvement state has also been studied with delayed
task-outcome feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
Continual Harness refines harnesses within continuous
episodes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karten et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, while concurrent methods HCL
and HSI study retention-aware adaptation and editable improvement
strategies, respectively <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou, 2026</a>)</cite>.
<span id="S5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="S5.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.p2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span> combines contrastive task-level refinement with updates to
the improvement harness in a one-pass deployment stream, without
ground-truth supervision or model updates. Table <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ A.1 Comparison Criteria ‣ Appendix A Comparison of Harness Improvement Methods ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> summarizes these distinctions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We presented <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="S6.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S6.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span>, a framework for recursive agent harnessing that
refines the current execution harness and learns from that process to
guide future harnessing. Task-level recursion builds on a contrastive
improvement step through parallel composition or sequential refinement;
harnessing-level recursion updates the improvement harness with adopted
harnesses and experience from their refinement.
Experiments across three professional benchmarks, two executors, and two
initial harnesses show consistent gains over the base configurations.
Ablation and transfer studies further demonstrate the value of scoped
proposals, composition, and accumulated harnessing experience. Together,
these findings support recursive harnessing as a way to turn task-specific
refinement into a reusable foundation for online test-time adaptation
across open-world tasks.</p>
</div>
