<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Comparison of Harness Improvement Methods</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Comparison Criteria</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ A.1 Comparison Criteria ‣ Appendix A Comparison of Harness Improvement Methods ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> compares six dimensions: <em id="A1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Object</em>,
<em id="A1.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Stage</em>, <em id="A1.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Label</em>, <em id="A1.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Streaming</em>, <em id="A1.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Scaling</em>, and
<em id="A1.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Improver Update</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Object.</span>
Automatic improvement ranges from prompts and
workflows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khattab et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agrawal et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>
to memories and skills <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2026</a>)</cite>,
and complete executable harnesses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
Component-level methods modify selected parts of an agent; harness-level
methods can jointly revise context management, tools, and control flow.
<span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="A1.SS1.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span> distinguishes the <em id="A1.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">execution harness</em>, which governs
task execution, from the <em id="A1.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">improvement harness</em>, which guides
the agent responsible for harness refinement.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage.</span>
Representative offline methods repeatedly evaluate candidate harnesses
on fixed benchmark tasks used as training or development
resources <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
Optimization targets a predefined task distribution before deployment.
These harnesses may transfer, but offline search does not incorporate
requirements first encountered during deployment.
Offline preparation can also support test-time adaptation (<em id="A1.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Both</em>):
MemoHarness builds a diagnostic experience bank for later case-specific
edits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>, while concurrent methods train
harness editors or generators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>.
<span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="A1.SS1.p3.1.3.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span> instead learns from arriving deployment tasks, starting with
an empty improvement library that is updated after each task.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Label.</span>
Ground-truth answers, task scores, and verifier outcomes provide
external correctness supervision. Model judgments and execution
traces can guide revision without establishing correctness.
RHI uses pairwise output judgments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>; TTHE uses
execution-derived proxies, including public tests when
available <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>.
<em id="A1.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Label</em> records external correctness feedback used for improvement
at either stage, excluding self-judgments and evaluation-only labels.
Label-free deployment therefore need not mean label-free preparation.
Feedback can also be delayed: Adaptive Auto-Harness uses outcomes
revealed after tasks resolve <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, but task completion
does not guarantee a subsequent correctness label.
<span id="A1.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="A1.SS1.p4.1.3.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A1.SS1.p4.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span> uses behavioral contrasts without immediate or delayed
correctness labels. Benchmark scores are reserved for evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Streaming.</span>
Online adaptation can process requests individually or in batches.
TTHE evaluates candidates on an unlabeled batch and carries the
selected harness into the next batch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>.
<em id="A1.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Streaming</em> includes such one-pass batch protocols as well as
task-by-task updates. Repeated execution of the current task or batch
is allowed, but replay of completed tasks is not. A single continuous
episode alone does not establish this task-stream protocol.
<span id="A1.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="A1.SS1.p5.1.3.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A1.SS1.p5.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span> processes tasks individually: future requests are unavailable,
completed tasks are not replayed, and the current task may be executed
repeatedly within its budget. Earlier harnesses and experience remain
available without rerunning their source tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scaling.</span>
Without correctness labels, additional test-time computation offers
a natural way to seek better performance through exploration and
self-assessment. This computation can generate alternative outputs,
refine trajectories, or search over harness changes.
ReasoningBank uses parallel and sequential exploration to improve
memory curation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>; Harness Scaling
allocates computation to task-specific harness
revision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>.
<span id="A1.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="A1.SS1.p6.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A1.SS1.p6.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span> scales harness refinement through parallel and sequential
recursion. Parallel recursion composes changes from multiple candidates
and their execution evidence; sequential recursion carries the refined
harness and accumulated evidence into the next step.
This exploration also supplies experience for the improvement harness;
we examine both current-task gains and benefits to future harnessing.
<em id="A1.SS1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Scaling</em> records an explicit test-time scaling study, not just
an adjustable iteration count. Performance gains alone do not establish
computational efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Improver Update.</span>
This column counts persistent updates to editor parameters, instructions,
or experience about generating and assessing revisions, beyond changes
to the execution artifact. Merely supplying the current artifact to
an editor does not count. Evo-Harness reads existing skills during
curation, but does not report retaining edit-process experience or
updating the curator’s strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
Meta-Harness and MemoHarness retain search or diagnostic experience
for later edits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>;
Adaptive Auto-Harness maintains a cross-cycle improvement workspace
with delayed outcome feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>;
concurrent methods train editors or revise evolution
strategies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou, 2026</a>)</cite>.
<em id="A1.SS1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Stage</em> and <em id="A1.SS1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Label</em> specify timing and supervision;
a check records an update mechanism, not verified gains in improvement ability.
<span id="A1.SS1.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="A1.SS1.p7.1.4.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A1.SS1.p7.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span> updates its improvement harness after each task using adopted
harnesses and lessons from candidate executions and composition decisions,
without correctness labels. Model weights and the improvement algorithm
remain fixed.
Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4 Analysis ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> tests whether greater task-level exploration
also improves future harnessing.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Our distinction lies in combining label-free task-stream refinement of
both harnesses with learning from task-level exploration, rather than
in streaming, experience reuse, or improver updates alone.</p>
</div>
