<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Benchmarks</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">WorkBuddy-Bench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2026</a>)</cite> contains three domains
as separate datasets: Code (<math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>80</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80</annotation></semantics></math> tasks over five role categories: developer,
algorithms, product, operations, and QA), Web (<math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>70</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70</annotation></semantics></math> tasks over seven
categories such as page interaction, data visualization, and document
conversion), and Office (<math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> tasks over data-file operations, document
operations, and workspace automation). Each dataset is split in half by
category, and we report on the test halves (<math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40/35/25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>35</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40/35/25</annotation></semantics></math> tasks). Tasks run in
the benchmark’s containers; the executor’s harness is mounted into the
container and every rollout is a fresh container. Grading is the benchmark’s
composite verifier: deterministic checks (hidden tests, file and structure
checks) combined with LLM and VLM judge items, with Gemini 3.5 Flash as the
judge model where one is needed; the score of a task is the fraction of its
checks that pass. The verifier never exposes its expected values to any
model in our pipeline.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">JobBench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026</a>)</cite> contains <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="65" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>65</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">65</annotation></semantics></math> professional
tasks, each with input documents and a set of requested deliverables, grouped
into seven occupational domains. We use a frozen <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="31/34" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>31</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>34</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">31/34</annotation></semantics></math> split and report on
the <math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="34" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>34</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">34</annotation></semantics></math> test tasks. Deliverables are judged against a weighted rubric by
Claude Opus 4.8; the task score is the total weighted score over the maximum
score, which is the benchmark’s own metric. The rubric is never
shown to the executor, the editor, or the selector.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LAB</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harvey AI, 2026</a>)</cite> is Harvey’s Legal Agent Benchmark:
<math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}251" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,251</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}251</annotation></semantics></math> legal tasks across <math id="A2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>24</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24</annotation></semantics></math> practice areas and four work types (analyse,
draft, review, research), each scored by an LLM judge against per-task
criteria, with no executable check. We follow the split of the
harness-optimization study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Niklaus, 2026</a>)</cite>, which applied
Meta-Harness to LAB and froze two disjoint sets: a <math id="A2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math>-task held-out
<em id="A2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">test</em> set, stratified by practice area and forced to cover all four
work types and the full range of context sizes (including one matter above
a million tokens), and a <math id="A2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>24</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24</annotation></semantics></math>-task <em id="A2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dev</em> set with one task per practice
area for fast iteration. Every LAB number in this paper is on the <math id="A2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> test
tasks, which each method processes as one stream; the <math id="A2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>24</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24</annotation></semantics></math> dev tasks are used
only by Meta-Harness as its search set. Tasks are judged by Claude Sonnet 4.6,
and we report the per-criterion pass rate (<em id="A2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Partial</em>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The train halves of WorkBuddy-Bench and JobBench are used by Meta-Harness
alone, as its offline setting. <span id="A2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="A2.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A2.p4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span> and the other baselines never
use them: they process the test tasks as a stream, starting from an empty
library or memory. Each reported score is the mean over three independent
streams, and #Steps is the number of model turns in the executor’s session.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Baselines</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All baselines use the same frozen executor and initial harness,
process the same test stream, and are evaluated by the same judge.
Revision and reflection use Claude Opus 4.8, matching our editor.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Best-of-<math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> We execute the base harness independently
<math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> times on each task. The same selector used by <span id="A3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="A3.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A3.p2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span> chooses one
deliverable to submit without access to scores.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ReasoningBank</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>. The harness
is never modified. Before each task, the memory items of the most similar
past task in the same domain (at most three, retrieved by embedding
similarity with <span id="A3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gemini-embedding-001</span>) are prepended to the task
prompt. After the task, an answer-free self-judge labels the rollout a success
or failure and the paper’s success and failure distillation prompts extract
up to three title–description–content items from the full trajectory into
the domain’s bank. At <math id="A3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> we use the paper’s
memory-aware test-time scaling: <math id="A3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> rollouts under the same retrieved memory,
best-of-<math id="A3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> selection over the deliverable text, and contrastive
distillation over all <math id="A3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> labelled trajectories.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Harness Scaling</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>. We port the
reference implementation’s per-task, verifier-blind revision loop to the
same editable components used by <span id="A3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="A3.p4.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A3.p4.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span>. For each task
in isolation: one base rollout as evidence; then, for <math id="A3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> iterations, a
single revision session that sees the task, every trajectory so far, and the
current tree, and must rewrite the tree (an empty edit is retried once); the
revised tree is rolled once as the next iteration’s evidence, and the tree
after the last iteration is deployed. There is no scope split, no
aggregation, no library, and the revision session never sees a score. At
<math id="A3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}1</annotation></semantics></math> this is one base rollout, one revision, one deployment. When the
deployment produces no output, the base run is submitted instead.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Meta-Harness</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>. The reference
implementation optimizes a harness offline and deploys it unchanged on the
test stream. Each iteration proposes candidate trees from the current
frontier, validates them, evaluates each on the whole search set, and
accepts the best only if it beats the frontier by a margin; every candidate,
accepted or not, is distilled into a lessons file the next round reads. On
WorkBuddy-Bench and JobBench, the search set is the training half pooled across categories
(<math id="A3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> iterations, <math id="A3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> candidates per iteration, <math id="A3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> replicates per task,
margin <math id="A3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math>); on LAB it is the <math id="A3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>24</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24</annotation></semantics></math>-task development set (<math id="A3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> iterations,
<math id="A3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> candidates, <math id="A3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> replicates, margin <math id="A3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Implementation Details</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We describe the executors and initial harnesses, improvement sessions,
and editable components used in our experiments.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Executors and Initial Harnesses</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The executor is a frozen model inside an off-the-shelf coding agent, and the
initial harness is that agent’s stock configuration extended with the
benchmark’s reference instructions. Under OpenCode <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">SST, 2024</a>)</cite> the
editable surface is the component tree of Table <a href="#A4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ D.3 Editable Harness Components ‣ Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>: four
always-on files (system prompt, guardrails, memory, tool guidance), skills
loaded on demand through the agent’s <span id="A4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">skill</span> tool with scripts carried
alongside them, subagents invoked through the <span id="A4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">task</span> tool, standalone
scripts run through the shell, and TypeScript plugins that act inside the
tool loop. Under Codex <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>)</cite> the always-on text lives in
<span id="A4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AGENTS.md</span> and the on-demand components are the same. In the stock
harness the always-on files are empty apart from the benchmark’s instructions
and every on-demand directory contains only a placeholder that names the
lever; the placeholder is what lets the editor discover a component exists.
Each rollout is capped at <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math> model turns and <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3{,}600" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3,600</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3{,}600</annotation></semantics></math> seconds
(WorkBuddy-Bench uses its container timeout at twice the benchmark default)
and runs with no memory of other tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Improvement Sessions and Library Configuration</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Roles.</span> The editor and the selector are Claude Opus 4.8
running as an agentic session under OpenCode at high reasoning effort. Every
session receives its evidence as files in a working directory rather than
inlined in a prompt. A guard refuses to hand
over any working directory that contains a grader verdict, rubric, or score.
A session that returns an invalid tree (a skill with no trigger sentence, a
script that does not compile, a helper a skill invokes but does not ship) is retried once with the validator’s
message; if it fails again the stage inherits the tree it started from, and
the fallback is recorded.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Library.</span> Each benchmark domain has its own library
directory. Tasks run sequentially within each domain and can read earlier
entries. An entry is the full adopted
tree of a task plus an <span id="A4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EXPERIENCE.md</span>,
which records why the harness was chosen and what recurring weakness it
addressed; an index lists every entry. Executable components are retained
rather than summarised as prose.</p>
</div>
</section>
