<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The full harness outperforms every ablation in all three domains.
Removing skills causes the largest loss on Code and Web (<math id="A5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.6</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.9</annotation></semantics></math> points), while removing scripts has the largest effect on Office
(<math id="A5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.4</annotation></semantics></math> points). On the three-domain average, the skills, scripts, and
prompt-group ablations lose <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.7</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5</annotation></semantics></math> points, respectively.
Every ablated harness still exceeds the base. The results support
contributions from multiple component groups, but do not establish
additive effects.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Reuse of Harnesses and Improvement Experience</h3>

<figure id="A5.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.14224v1/library_reuse.svg" id="A5.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/135;" width="476" height="135"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>: </span><span id="A5.F10.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Library provenance and the distribution of edits.<span id="A5.F10.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Left: modified components compared with the library snapshot available
during refinement. Containment measures the share of non-boilerplate
lines found in a library entry for the same component. Lines common to
more than a fifth of a benchmark’s adopted harnesses are excluded.
Right: edit distribution across components.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">What the Library Carries.</span>
Figure <a href="#A5.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ E.2 Reuse of Harnesses and Improvement Experience ‣ Appendix E Supplementary Experiments and Analyses ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> compares each modified component against
the library snapshot available during refinement. Library provenance is
present in <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>22</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22\%</annotation></semantics></math> of modified files on LAB and WB-office and <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="47\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>47</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">47\%</annotation></semantics></math> on
JobBench. Only <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%</annotation></semantics></math> of modified files retain at least half their
lines from an entry, indicating that adaptation is more common than
extensive copying. Skills and scripts account for roughly half the
edits on each benchmark. These measurements show how existing harnesses
are reused alongside the experience that guides their refinement.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F11" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:60%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.14224v1/loo_library.svg" id="A5.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/136;" width="286" height="136"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F11.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 11</span>: </span><span id="A5.F11.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Leave-one-out reuse.<span id="A5.F11.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Each task is re-run with its
own entry excluded from the completed library. Annotations show gains
over the original online run at <math id="A5.F11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Leave-One-Out Re-run.</span>
After the stream completes, each task is re-run with its own entry
removed from the library (Figure <a href="#A5.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ E.2 Reuse of Harnesses and Improvement Experience ‣ Appendix E Supplementary Experiments and Analyses ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>). Scores rise
from <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="77.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>77.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">77.5</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="79.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>79.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">79.4</annotation></semantics></math> on WorkBuddy-Bench and from <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="36.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>36.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">36.0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="37.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>37.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">37.7</annotation></semantics></math> on
JobBench. The re-run can access entries from both earlier and later
tasks, so this is an offline reuse analysis rather than an estimate
of online gains. Repeated execution may also contribute to
the difference.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.3 </span>Transfer Across Executor Models</h3>

<div id="A5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We test whether a harness refined for one executor remains useful
when deployed with a different model, without further adaptation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F12" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:60%;"><img src="2610.14224v1/figures/cross_model_transfer.png" id="A5.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/117;" width="286" height="117" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F12.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 12</span>: </span><span id="A5.F12.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Cross-model transfer on WorkBuddy-Bench.<span id="A5.F12.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The harness
refined for Gemini 3.5 Flash under OpenCode is applied unchanged to
GLM-5, Gemini 3.7 Flash, and Claude Opus 4.8, and compared with each
executor’s base harness. Annotations show the score gain.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A5.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ E.3 Transfer Across Executor Models ‣ Appendix E Supplementary Experiments and Analyses ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> applies a harness refined for
Gemini 3.5 Flash to three other executors without further adaptation.
All three improve on both Code and Office. GLM-5 gains <math id="A5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>17.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17.3</annotation></semantics></math> points
on Code (<math id="A5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="58.5\!\to\!75.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>75.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">58.5\!\to\!75.8</annotation></semantics></math>), while Gemini 3.7 Flash gains <math id="A5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.3</annotation></semantics></math> on
Code and <math id="A5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.4</annotation></semantics></math> on Office. Opus 4.8 gains <math id="A5.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.6</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.1</annotation></semantics></math> points.
The gains show that adopted harnesses can transfer across models,
although the benefit varies substantially with the executor and
domain. This complements the same-model refinement experiment in
Figure <a href="#S4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 4 Analysis ‣ Harness2: Recursive Agent Harnessing foran Open World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
