<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cost of recursive refinement.</span>
<span id="A6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Harness<sup id="A6.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A6.p1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></sup></span> requires additional executions and editor sessions to propose,
probe, and compose harness changes. Increasing the recursion budget
therefore adds inference cost. Parallel sessions can reduce latency when
resources permit, but do not eliminate this additional computation.
Our experiments characterize this trade-off over a bounded range of
budgets rather than establishing a universally optimal allocation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Execution-based feedback.</span>
Without ground-truth supervision, refinement relies on behavioral
contrasts and model judgment. These signals help assess the effects
of a proposed change, but remain sensitive to execution variability
and shared model biases. They do not guarantee correctness or establish
that an adopted change will transfer to a different task.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Future Directions</h2>

<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Managing the improvement harness.</span>
The current improvement harness retains adopted harnesses and experience
in a simple indexed library. As this library grows, future work could
consolidate overlapping components, revise outdated experience, and
refine retrieval according to the current task and available evidence.
Such management could keep the improvement harness compact and relevant
while preserving the context needed to reuse earlier discoveries.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adaptive recursion.</span>
We currently specify the recursion form and budget in advance. Future
work could allocate exploration according to task difficulty and the
relevance of prior experience, or stop when additional probes yield
little new evidence. This could balance the cost of current refinement
with the value of the experience it provides for future harnessing.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A8" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix H </span>Prompts</h2>

<div id="A8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section lists the prompts of the harness improver: the Harness Editor
(Prompts <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span> and <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>), the Harness Composer
(Prompts <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span> and <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>), the final selection
(Prompt <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>) and the Experience Reflector (Prompt <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>). Every
improver session is agentic: the model is started inside a workspace whose <span id="A8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">context/</span>
directory holds the task, the current harness, the full trajectories, the produced deliverables
and the library, and the prompt says what to do with that evidence rather than inlining it.
The prompts below are the part shared by every benchmark. Each benchmark appends a short block
of its own describing the work, the tools its bridge exposes, the fields of its environment
view, and its domain-specific constraints; those blocks are in the code release. The two Editor
prompts are identical except for their dimension section, so the invariant prompt is printed
whole and only the domain dimension section follows it; the sequential Composer differs from
the parallel one in four passages, which are printed alone; and the Composer’s last four
sections are identical to the Editor’s, so they are printed once, in Prompt <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>.
The component surface shown is the one for the OpenCode executor harness; the Codex variant
differs only in the component paths it lists. Scale symbols <math id="A8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> are
substituted with the session’s integers at run time. No prompt names a rubric, score or
grader; each states that no such thing exists in its context.</p>
</div>
